  </section>

  <section class="individual text-left pb-3 pt-5" id="step3">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="row">
        <div class="col-sm-12">
          <div class="title-container">
            <img src="https://individualogist.com/soul-flame-tarot/assets/img/title-bg-v2.png" alt="Snow" style="width:100%;" class="long-title">
            <h3 class="centered">As A Member, These Are Your <span class="text-orange">Relationship Qualities</span> That Are Within You</h3>
          </div>
          <div class="row">
            <div class="col-sm-4">
              <img src="../assets/images/relationship.png" style="width: 100%;">
            </div>
            <div class="col-sm-8">
              <p>Due to your we-oriented nature, compromises are part of your romantic game. Whereas other archetypes tend to be dominant or suppressive, you are inclined to negotiations and dialogues. </p>
              <p>When you are in a relationship, this tendency will bring equitability. As such, you won’t put much emphasis on gender roles. Whereas some (or even most) would perceive that men should belong to the garage and women to the kitchen, your progressive mindset sees beyond these pre-disposed boundaries. </p>
              <p>Given this, you are likely to will look at things from a fairer standpoint, one that does not seek to discriminate but to compromise. When you are assigned with cooking, you’ll expect your partner to do the dishes, or vice versa. </p>
              <p>And when facing uphill battles, you’ll tend to <span class="text-orange">seek a diplomatic solution</span>. Undeniably, relationships are comprised of two different individuals, both of which are unique. You recognize this just as how you see life’s paradox. Thus, you won’t force the issue and seek for a middle-ground where both can agree on something. </p>

            </div>
          </div>
          <p>Be careful, though. Sometimes people perceive compromises as weakness. For instance, a partner whose possessive will exploit your diplomatic tendencies. They might present unfair conditions to manipulate outcomes. </p>
          <p>But as a Member with a keen eye, you’ll be able to see through these ulterior motives. After all, if you’ve lived true to your archetype, you’re definitely a people-person. While issues may arise, your compromising nature isn’t simply there to yield. Instead, its presence is the predicament to agreement. </p>
          <p>Always remember that this tendency, when used correctly, is a strength not a weakness. Compromises often build <span class="text-orange">innate trust and collaborative communication</span>. Coupled with the right partners, this tendency will pave the way for a progressive union. </p>
          <hr class="dashed">
          <p>Looking at the core qualities of your archetype, we’ll see a Member whose happiness depends on how they align with their partners. Given how you prioritize unity over individuality, it is to be expected that you’ll seek a kind of relationship that’s centered on reciprocity and cooperation. Thus, you’ll likely become co-dependent with your partner. </p>
          <p>This trait often manifests in your likelihood to see <span class="text-orange">interdependence as the focal point</span> of any relationship. Because you seek to attain the highest form of unity with your partner, it is unavoidable for things to become interdependent as you share your life with them. </p>
          <p>For instance, you are that type of partner who probably can’t move alone. When going to the grocery or hardware stores, you make sure that your partner is with you. Since you are co-dependent with them, being physically separated with your significant other creates problems, even when you don’t really admit them to yourself.</p>
          <p>To an extent, you feel this way because you find security in them. Just as how a Member must belong to a group or a community, you need that sense of belongingness in order to become the best version of yourself. </p>
          <p>As such, your significant other will eventually become someone you can’t do away with. Be careful, though. Your partner is still an individual who has their own needs and wants in life. While they may decide to shape their life with you, remember that they also need to manifest their own autonomy in order to <span class="text-orange">recognize their worth</span>. And while you may not intend it, gluing yourself to them might actually hinder their personal growth.</p>
          <p>As you know, however, the Member is not just about romance and love. Indeed, there’s another aspect in your life where you can apply yourself, <span class="username"></span>. </p>
          <p>Without further ado, it’s time to see where you can take yourself in your career.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="col-sm-12 text-center">
          <a class="btn btn-outline-success btn-3" id="nav-btn2" onclick="nextReading(3,4)">Show Me Now</a>
        </div>
